Exactly. The mathematically interesting object there is the intersection of operators.

For example, addition and multiplication happen to agree at a particular point:

2+2=4

and

2\times2=4

So someone could use the wrong operator and still produce the correct observation. But move one step away:

4+4=8,\qquad 4\times4=16

and the hidden difference suddenly becomes visible.

More formally, ask where

x+x=x^2.

That gives

x^2-2x=0

so

x(x-2)=0,

meaning the two rules intersect only at x=0 and x=2. Agreement at those points tells you almost nothing about whether the underlying rule is addition or multiplication.

That gives us a very useful Pantheon primitive: operator intersections.

T_1(x)=T_2(x)

at some x, even though

T_1\neq T_2.

Those points are simultaneously powerful and dangerous. Powerful because they reveal a structural connection between otherwise different systems. Dangerous because they can create false confidence: “My explanation predicted the answer!” Yes—but perhaps several incompatible explanations predicted that exact answer.

And that connects directly to humor. A lot of humor exploits an apparent path that suddenly reveals itself to have been generated by a different operator:

\text{expected relation}
\rightarrow
\text{unexpected relation}
\rightarrow
\text{retroactive coherence}.

The punchline makes you realize, Oh—that connection works too.

Not every joke works because it is literally true, but many satisfying ones expose a relationship that becomes obvious only after the alternate framing appears. The pleasure is partly in discovering the hidden intersection.

And this gives you a better experimental maneuver than merely looking for matches:

\boxed{\text{Find the intersection, then perturb around it.}}

If two explanations both account for x, test x-\epsilon and x+\epsilon.

That is how you distinguish:

coincidence from invariant,
analogy from shared mechanism,
one-point agreement from generalizable operator.

And that is where your “primes” become useful. I would broaden the existing prime concept slightly: a Pantheon prime needn’t be a thing that means exactly the same thing everywhere. It can be a minimal relational junction that survives enough transformations to be recombined elsewhere.

Something like:

P =
\text{smallest structure worth preserving across translations}.

Then you can factor richer phenomena into them:

\text{phenomenon}
\approx
P_1\circ P_2\circ P_3\circ\dots

but crucially, you preserve the operators between the primes. Otherwise you make precisely your 4+4 versus 4\times4 mistake.

So the representation shouldn’t merely say:

\{P_1,P_2,P_3\}.

It needs:

P_1
\overset{T_a}{\longrightarrow}
P_2
\overset{T_b}{\longrightarrow}
P_3.

Dots plus the rules connecting the dots.

That may actually be one of the safeguards the broader Pantheon needs: never transfer a discovered “prime” cross-domain without carrying at least some information about which operator produced it, where that operator was tested, and where competing operators intersect it.

Because the funniest—and scientifically most interesting—places can be exactly where two completely different pieces of math accidentally give the same answer.

Those are junctions worth marking on the map.